# mailx — real-world examples

## Send a file as an attachment, interactively

```
mailx -a ~/file1 user@domain.com
Subject: sending file1                      # mailx prompts for a subject
~c david_haines@domain.com bwdave@domain.com   # ~c = CC, ~b = BCC
Hello,

This is the body of the email

Thank you
~< !cat ~/signature       # run a command, append its stdout to the message
EOT                        # Ctrl+D to send
```

## Set the From: address

```sh
mailx -r noreply@homelab.lan -s "report" user@domain.com < report.txt
```

## Non-interactive one-liner (great for scripts)

```sh
echo "backup completed $(date)" | mailx -s "backup OK" bwdave@protonmail.com
```

## Homelab: mail a log excerpt after a failed service

```sh
journalctl -u plexmediaserver -n 50 --no-pager | \
  mailx -s "plex failed on mediaserver" bwdave@protonmail.com
```

## Homelab: install on Tumbleweed

```sh
sudo zypper install mailx
```

## Tilde escapes (while composing)

| Escape   | What it does                            |
| -------- | --------------------------------------- |
| ~c addr  | add CC recipient                        |
| ~b addr  | add BCC recipient                       |
| ~s text  | set/replace the subject                 |
| ~< !cmd  | append output of a shell command        |
| ~v       | open the message in $EDITOR             |
| ~q       | abort the message                       |

| Flag | What it does        |
| ---- | ------------------- |
| -s   | subject             |
| -a   | attach a file       |
| -r   | From: address       |
| -c   | CC list             |
